<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Donegal Friary</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1474">1474</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Donegal Abbey</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-donegal">Donegal</a> Friary was a Franciscan house founded in 1474 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-hugh-odonnell">Hugh Roe O'Donnell</a>, lord of Tír Chonaill, and his wife Nuala at Donegal town. It was the family's chief religious foundation and burial place. Friars of Donegal later compiled the Annals of the Four Masters in the 1630s, after the friary itself had been ruined in the wars of the 1590s and 1601.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/donegal-friary.jpg" alt="Donegal Friary" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andreas F. Borchert · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Donegal_Friary_Cloister_East_Range_2009_09_23.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
